From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom: q6v5: wake the system up on a watchdog bite

The watchdog interrupt is a separate interrupt, not an SMP2P one, and it
is not a wakeup interrupt. A watchdog bite while the system is suspended
is only handled at the next resume, and the remote processor is dead
until then.

The downstream kernel (subsys-pil-tz) arms the watchdog bite interrupt
for wakeup while the subsystem is running.

Register the watchdog interrupt as the device's wake IRQ. The PM core
arms it during suspend when the device may wake the system up. The PAS
driver already enables wakeup for its device, to hold off suspend during
a recovery, so a watchdog bite of a PAS remote processor now wakes the
system up. Nothing changes for the other drivers until their wakeup is
enabled.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
Built with W=1 and dtbs_check; not tested on the device yet.

diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
index fe148b4..82cc65b 100644
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 #include <linux/interconnect.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/pm_wakeirq.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/qcom_aoss.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/smem.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/smem_state.h>
@@ -305,6 +306,14 @@ int qcom_q6v5_init(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5, struct platform_device *pdev,
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * A watchdog bite while the system is suspended should start the
+	 * recovery right away, if the remoteproc device may wake the system.
+	 */
+	ret = devm_pm_set_wake_irq(&pdev->dev, q6v5->wdog_irq);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	q6v5->fatal_irq = platform_get_irq_byname(pdev, "fatal");
 	if (q6v5->fatal_irq < 0)
 		return q6v5->fatal_irq;
